{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RhoaTZkziySLCDraiBDKuR","type":"tool_result","content":"e181e6f base\n---\n---\n\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\n\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\n\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\neveryone.\n\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\n\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose renderer lacks that font\n  glyph. I drew the checkmark as an inline SVG `<path>` instead — guaranteed\n  everywhere.\n- **GitHub proxies and caches your image** (its \"camo\" layer). The count is\n  live at the source, but on a README it refreshes within the cache window, not\n  instantly — so send a sane `Cache-Control` and don't promise real-time.\n\nThe result: every item and every author now has a badge. I surfaced a\n---\nsite/app.js:7:      const r = await fetch(`/v1/notes/${m[1]}`);\nsite/app.js:13:      const r = await fetch(\"/v1/notes?limit=50\");\n---\nNOTES.md:4:robots.txt and the new notes.\nnotes/soft-404-on-pages-that-load-fine.md:3:# Soft 404 on pages that load fine: my robots.txt blocked the API my pages render from\nnotes/soft-404-on-pages-that-load-fine.md:11:My pages are rendered by JavaScript from my own API at `/v1/...`, and Google renders them the same way. On September 7 I had added `Disallow: /v1/` to robots.txt, to stop crawlers hitting POST-only API addresses that appear as text on story pages and logging 404s. Google obeys robots.txt when it renders too. So it opened the story, wasn't allowed to fetch the story's data, and when that fetch fails my app shows its \"NOT FOUND — No story lives at this address\" screen. Google read that as a page that doesn't exist. The graph lines up with the date: pages crawled before September 7 stayed indexed, and everything after was turned away.\nnotes/soft-404-on-pages-that-load-fine.md:15:The fix was robots.txt only: allow the public read endpoints the pages need, and keep the POST-only and per-user ones blocked with longer, more specific rules. Google uses the most specific matching rule, so `Disallow: /v1/stories/*/reproduced` still beats `Allow: /v1/stories`.\nnotes/soft-404-on-pages-that-load-fine.md:21:Prerequisites: a site whose pages are filled in by JavaScript from an API on the same domain (React, Vue, vanilla JS, anything), a verified Google Search Console property, and access to the site's `robots.txt`.\nnotes/soft-404-on-pages-that-load-fine.md:26:1. Fetch `https://<site>/robots.txt`. List every `Disallow` rule that matches an address your page's JavaScript fetches while it renders (open the page with the browser's Network tab, or grep the app's source for `fetch(` / `axios`). Any match is a candidate cause: Googlebot renders the page without that data.\nnotes/soft-404-on-pages-that-load-fine.md:28:3. Rewrite robots.txt with Google's longest-match rule in mind: `Allow` the public read endpoints the pages need (`Allow: /v1/stories`), keep the general `Disallow: /v1/`, and re-block anything that must stay out with a longer rule (`Disallow: /v1/stories/*/reproduced`). Never unblock per-user or admin endpoints.\nnotes/soft-404-on-pages-that-load-fine.md:30:5. Deploy, then confirm the live file: `curl -s https://<site>/robots.txt`.\nnotes/soft-404-on-pages-that-load-fine.md:32:7. In Search Console: Settings → Crawling → robots.txt → Open report → ⋮ on your host → Request a recrawl. Then URL Inspection → Test live URL on the affected page, and Request indexing (about 10 URLs/day).\nsite/index.html:6:<meta name=\"robots\" content=\"index,follow\">","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5adf69f5-e7dd-47a4-a61b-14003a665e05","uuid":"14d70032-7b26-4bda-943a-df92a65a9804","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:03:10.729Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"e181e6f base\n---\n---\n\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\n\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\n\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\neveryone.\n\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\n\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose